  "body": "Regression from PR #1546 (#1536, merged 5aab848f): the per-face tessellation evaluation cap `FACE_EVAL_CAP` (25 M, `crates/app/src/geometry/import/step_guard.rs:45`) falsely refuses healthy STEP models.\n\n**Evidence** (M5's review of #1546, pullrequestreview-5898774979, posted after the merge):\n- Corpus: 75 STEP files (testdata plus a user's project library). Measured peak evaluations on one face in one pass: 32050.STEP 23.1 M (**92 % of the cap**), socket-head screws 16-19 M, 10208 / 22588B 7.3 M. A healthy face can need far more than the \"a few million\" the code comment and `docs/decisions/1536-step-tess-guard.md` claim.\n- **A false refusal:** `10208.STEP` at the user-settable facet budget of 60 M (`step_budget::CEILING`). On main before #1546 it imports (14,866,333 facets at chord 5e-3, 173 s). After #1546 it is refused as \"did not converge\" on face #1073, which actually converged at 28.07 M evaluations.\n- **Why the whole model fails:** the trip returns `Err(Step)` through `step_refine::refine`, which refuses the whole model. Before, a failed finer pass kept the coarser mesh.\n\n**Expected:**\n- A face that converges is never refused at any allowed budget. Give the cap real headroom (e.g. about 10x; about 12-15 s on a broken face in release), or scale it with the facet budget.\n- When a finer refinement pass trips the cap, keep the coarser pass's mesh (with a note) instead of refusing the whole model, as before #1546.\n- The truly non-converging face (the #1536 case) is still refused by name in bounded time.\n\n**Done means:** 10208.STEP imports at the 60 M budget as before; a pin shows that a face converging past the old cap is not refused; the #1536 synthetic and sc_logo cases still refuse by name within bounded time; and the comment and decision file carry the measured corpus numbers.\n\nRelated: #1536, PR #1546, #1545, #1548.\n",
